PyDoc_STRVAR(__pyx_doc_6classy_5Class_56get_transfer_and_k_and_z, "\n        Returns a dictionary of grids of density and/or velocity transfer function values and the z and k at which it has been fully computed.\n        Useful for creating interpolators.\n        When setting CLASS input parameters, include at least one of \047dTk\047 (for density transfer functions) or \047vTk\047 (for velocity transfer functions).\n        Following the default output_format=\047class\047, all transfer functions will be normalised to \047curvature R=1\047 at initial time\n        (and not \047curvature R = -1/k^2\047 like in CAMB).\n        You may switch to output_format=\047camb\047 for the CAMB definition and normalisation of transfer functions.\n        (Then, \047dTk\047 must be in the input: the CAMB format only outputs density transfer functions).\n        When sticking to output_format=\047class\047, you also get the newtonian metric fluctuations phi and psi.\n        If you set the CLASS input parameter \047extra_metric_transfer_functions\047 to \047yes\047,\n        you get additional metric fluctuations in the synchronous and N-body gauges.\n\n        Parameters\n        ----------\n        output_format  : (\047class\047 or \047camb\047)\n                Format transfer functions according to CLASS (default) or CAMB\n        h_units : bool\n                Whether the units of k in output are h/Mpc or 1/Mpc (default)\n\n        Returns\n        -------\n        tk : dictionary containing all transfer functions.\n                For instance, the grid of values of \047d_c\047 (= delta_cdm) is available in tk[\047d_c\047]\n                All these grids have indices [index_k,index,z], for instance tk[\047d_c\047][index_k,index,z]\n        k : vector of k values (in units of 1/Mpc by default, or h/Mpc when setting h_units to True)\n        z : vector of z values\n        ");
